# US English as a merge of IBM's 4.3 and ETI's 6.1: one decision a line,
# what was done, where, to what, and why. Everything not named here is 4.3 as
# it was, or 6.1 already, taken earlier through the punctuation and the text
# normaliser (docs/quirks.md has those).
#
# The evidence is tools/measure/referee.py, which holds both engines' sounds
# against the CMU Pronouncing Dictionary. Where only one engine's sounds are
# CMU's, that one is taken. Where both are, or neither, a word one engine's
# dictionary holds and the other's does not keeps the dictionary's reading --
# a pronunciation someone wrote down beats one a rule guessed -- and anything
# else goes to 6.1, which is what most ears know as Eloquence.
#
# Where the referee could not settle it -- a foreign name read natively by
# IBM and anglicised by CMU and 6.1, or a word whose stress alone differs --
# both readings were listened to and one chosen, and the line says so.
#
#   drop     the entry is taken out of that dictionary
#   add      the word is put into it, saying what 6.1 says
#   set      what it says is changed to what 6.1 says, or a lookup set's
#            words to 6.1's
#   read-as  the word is read as the text given, which 6.1 does
#   letter   an arm added to that letter in lang/enus/letters
#   rule     a rule of 6.1's lifted in place of IBM's
#
# test/cases/words-enus.txt holds every word here, so test/words.sh says if
# any of them moves.

